    },
    {
      "id": "36yCyOJKOizfumN4I30fs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 22055.251458084516,
      "y": -14523.956001726574,
      "width": 1154.9996337890625,
      "height": 1820,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1381117797,
      "version": 3039,
      "versionNonce": 256554923,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1764176666508,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Scalability: \n    - shard the data across multiple node\n\n2. What about reliability and durability ? \n    - replicate the data to other node or \n      other data centre\n    - Approach \n        - replicate data across these node \n        - may be some k nodes. \n        - one will be leader other will be like\n          follower\n        - here we have approach like master-slave , or master-master -> \n          cassandra/dynamodb work on maaster-master\n\n3. what about consistency ? \n    - this is tough problem. We can use quorem for read r, write w, total n\n        - Strong consistent:\n            - read -> last update write alwasy\n        - weak consistent\n            - may not see the latest.\n        - eventual:\n            - may not see the latest, but eventually will have.\n\n        - r+w > n -> consistent.\n        - r = 1, w = n -> read fast write slow.\n        - r = n , w = 1 -> write fast read slow.\n        - r+w <= n -> no 100% consistency guarantee.\n    \n\n4. What about distributed consensus issues.\n    - node got fail \n        - we can maintain the heart beat of each node with \n          gossip protocol.\n        - in case some node is down, each node will be aware of that\n        - and in our definition, of k -> we can assume \n          working k nodes not failed. \n    - node returning some wrong info, basically node is fine\n      but malfunctioned. ( Byzantine failure ) \n        - this problem is tough to solve. \n        - we do have some stand algorithm to solve distrbiteud\n            consensus algorithms -> Raft, paxos etc. \n        - raft -> Leader based algorithm -> for write \n\n5. How we are resolving conflict in case of weak or eventually \n   consistency\n    - in case of strong consistency -> you can have value as per voting.\n    - in case of weak its tough, which to believe. \n    - there are multiple ways one is like do LWW, aws dynamodb uses this \n      to upda\n    - and fanout to other nodes for eventual consistency.\n   \n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Scalability: \n    - shard the data across multiple node\n\n2. What about reliability and durability ? \n    - replicate the data to other node or \n      other data centre\n    - Approach \n        - replicate data across these node \n        - may be some k nodes. \n        - one will be leader other will be like\n          follower\n        - here we have approach like master-slave , or master-master -> \n          cassandra/dynamodb work on maaster-master\n\n3. what about consistency ? \n    - this is tough problem. We can use quorem for read r, write w, total n\n        - Strong consistent:\n            - read -> last update write alwasy\n        - weak consistent\n            - may not see the latest.\n        - eventual:\n            - may not see the latest, but eventually will have.\n\n        - r+w > n -> consistent.\n        - r = 1, w = n -> read fast write slow.\n        - r = n , w = 1 -> write fast read slow.\n        - r+w <= n -> no 100% consistency guarantee.\n    \n\n4. What about distributed consensus issues.\n    - node got fail \n        - we can maintain the heart beat of each node with \n          gossip protocol.\n        - in case some node is down, each node will be aware of that\n        - and in our definition, of k -> we can assume \n          working k nodes not failed. \n    - node returning some wrong info, basically node is fine\n      but malfunctioned. ( Byzantine failure ) \n        - this problem is tough to solve. \n        - we do have some stand algorithm to solve distrbiteud\n            consensus algorithms -> Raft, paxos etc. \n        - raft -> Leader based algorithm -> for write \n\n5. How we are resolving conflict in case of weak or eventually \n   consistency\n    - in case of strong consistency -> you can have value as per voting.\n    - in case of weak its tough, which to believe. \n    - there are multiple ways one is like do LWW, aws dynamodb uses this \n      to upda\n    - and fanout to other nodes for eventual consistency.\n   \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "TWJKMIc48nr6GNEZ2z6aM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 19261.169820934214,
      "y": -14646.550389710734,
      "width": 1278.1995849609375,
      "height": 490,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1P",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1887688645,
      "version": 586,
      "versionNonce": 896335077,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1764175959247,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Read -> \n\n    - we can maintain bloom filter\n    - we can maintain inmemory\n    - else search in disk. -> data node level\n    - for making this write heavy -> we can use concept like SStables and LSM tree \n    - for read heavy can go with b+tree\n\n\nWrite -> \n    - write in append only logs.\n    - write in inmemory\n    - it will be flushed asyn.\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Read -> \n\n    - we can maintain bloom filter\n    - we can maintain inmemory\n    - else search in disk. -> data node level\n    - for making this write heavy -> we can use concept like SStables and LSM tree \n    - for read heavy can go with b+tree\n\n\nWrite -> \n    - write in append only logs.\n    - write in inmemory\n    - it will be flushed asyn.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
